    {
      "idx": 1,
      "label": "2",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "The chain now runs without hand-written code for a new family: proposer writes, verification accepts, a 0.6B caller trained on other families calls it. Stress the caveat: MRCR is a parsing task in disguise, so this is program discovery. The ladder: the 8B and a 4-bit 14B, both thinking, each succeed on 2 of 5 seeds; scale does not make discovery reliable, though the 14B succeeds sooner when it does. Every trained 4B fails."
    },
    {
      "idx": 2,
      "label": "3",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "Generated families give resolution MRCR cannot: 37 held-out families per proposer. Thinking and scale both matter. SFT on the 8B's verified code plus 40 steps of RL with the verified teaching score as reward lifts a 4B from 9 to 30 of 74 (replicated). The caution of the day: round four's 42, level with the 8B, was the best of four RL seeds of the same recipe (22, 29, 33, 42). With three times the helper-family teacher data the recipe averages 33.7 over three seeds (32 to 36), matches round four on solves, and reaches the 8B on helper families. Recipe comparisons need several seeds; checkpoints should be chosen on held-out discovery. MRCR still fails."
    },
    {
      "idx": 3,
      "label": "4",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "The day's main result, tested prospectively and replicated with a second bootstrap seed (pooled 26 against 29 of 60, one false acceptance in 60). On 30 families neither proposer had seen, a 4B trained on the 8B's verified libraries, sampling eight candidates and accepting by the three worked examples in its own prompt, bootstraps as many correct libraries as the thinking 8B with the full protocol and thirty labels, at a fourteenth of the tokens and with no false acceptances. The mechanism is the label curve: on exact families a program that reproduces three examples and runs cleanly on the other inputs is almost always right. The limit matters: on noisy families (MuSiQue F1, OOLONG classification) a few labels cannot rank close candidates."
    },
    {
      "idx": 4,
      "label": "5",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "The cleanest result of the day. Same code, four docstrings. The usage line alone carries the effect. The practical rule: every library ships with a usage line; the bootstrapper now inserts one. The deeper point: an untrained caller reads documentation, a distilled caller has learned the call."
    },
    {
      "idx": 5,
      "label": "6",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "MuSiQue is where the routine needs helper calls. The two-round library wins on accuracy and cost through the 0.6B caller. Everything that adds capacity elsewhere (recursion, bigger reader, more samples) does not help. The gap to gold paragraphs (about 0.2) is evidence selection."
    },
    {
      "idx": 6,
      "label": "7",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "LongBench-v2 is the external-validity check. The first generic library (map-reduce notes) tied direct reading at nine times the cost; a retrieval library (BM25 over passages for the question and each option, one read) beats direct reading on all 503 questions, 0.427 against 0.356 (+0.07, se 0.02), at a quarter of its tokens, also through the 0.6B caller; the first 150-question sample had suggested +0.11. The lesson matches MuSiQue: choose evidence, do not summarize everything. Two earlier readings were withdrawn after fresh samples. The method lessons are the ones that cost most: scripted harnesses that did not match the kernel, verification sets that did not span deployment lengths."
    },
    {
      "idx": 7,
      "label": "8",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "The sharpened thesis in one breath, then the four next steps. If time is short, skip the list and ask the advisor which of the four matters most for the thesis."
    },
    {
      "idx": 8,
      "label": "9",
      "overlay": 0,
      "note": "For questions only."
    }
  ]
}
